{
 "title": "Basic shaders part 1: a studio and a material library",
 "summary": "The studio the tutor saves as the startup file for every material chapter: a floor plane scaled out, its back edge extruded up into a wall and the corner bevelled into a curve (smooth by angle), dark grey and rough; one of Blender's own HDRIs (interior) for light, a lamp and a pink one; a straight-on camera (X 0, rotation 90/0/0) locked, passepartout 1. Then the material library, each marked as an asset: chrome (metallic 1, roughness 0.05), brushed aluminium (anisotropic 1), dark grey metal, gold (hex D4AF37), glossy green (roughness 0, not metallic), glossy green painted (a copy at roughness 0.25). Beyond the tutorial: copper by the same hex trick. The node wrangler's Ctrl+T (texture coordinate + mapping + image) is what edit_nodes builds directly.",
